\c 1
\s1 Empress Vashti refuses Emperor Ahasuerus
\rem /s1 King Ahasuerus's banquet; Queen Vashti Deposed; Queen Vashti Defies King Xerxes; King Ahasuerus Deposes Queen Vashti; The King's Banquet
\p
\v 1 The following events happened during¦228502 the¦228502 reign¦228506 of¦228502 \add Emperor\add* Ahasuerus¦228503.\x + \xo 1:1: \xt Ezr 4:6.\x* (\add Also known as King Xerxes,\add* \add *he¦228505\add* ruled¦228506 the 127 provinces¦228514 \add of Persia extending\add* from¦228507 India¦228507 to¦228508 Ethiopia¦228510.)
\v 2 In¦228516 those¦228517 days¦228516, \add *he¦228521\add* ruled his¦228524 empire from Shushan¦228526,\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Known as ‘Susa’ in many 20th century translations, even though earlier English translations often got it correct.\f* city.
\p
\v 3 In¦228529 the third year¦228529 of¦228531 his¦228531 reign¦228531, he¦228532 hosted a¦228533 celebration for all¦228534 of¦228536 his¦228536 officials and¦228537 for every important person who worked for him, the wealthy landowners, the officials of¦228537 the¦228544 provinces¦228544, as well as the officers who served in the¦228538 army¦228538 of¦228538 the \add combined empire\add* of Persia¦228540 and¦228541 Media¦228541. He was¦228545 present in person
\v 4 \add to host\add* the six-month celebration which, displayed¦228547 the¦228550 incredible wealth¦228550 and prestige of his¦228552 empire and¦228553 his¦228557 personal wealth and¦228561 power.
\p
\v 5 At the¦228566 end¦228564 of that time¦228566, the emperor¦228569 hosted \add another\add* celebration, but this time for all¦228570 the¦228572 people¦228572 in the royal fort in¦228574 Shushan¦228574, from the¦228575 nobles to¦228576 the least¦228579 prestigious. He held this celebration over an entire week in¦228583 the enclosed garden¦228584 of¦228584 the¦228585 royal palace¦228585.
\v 6 There were white¦228588 and¦228591 blue curtains hanging from white and¦228596 purple¦228596 cords¦228593 attached to silver rings on marble pillars¦228601. The guests reclined on gold¦228605 and¦228606 silver¦228606 couches¦228603 set on¦228607 a¦228608 mosaic floor made of¦228608 red and¦228611 white marble¦228611, mother-of-pearl¦228612 and¦228613 other expensive \add #stones¦228613\add*.
\v 7 Drinks¦228615 were served in¦228616 golden¦228617 goblets, and¦228618 each one was¦228623 a unique design. The¦228625 king's¦228625 wine¦228621 seemed limitless as he displayed his \add ≈generosity\add*,
\v 8 and it was¦228628 served according¦228628 to his rule: there¦228629 was¦228629 no compulsion to drink¦228627, but his¦228641 servants were to¦228643 give each guest however much they wanted.
\p
\v 9 Meanwhile, Queen¦228650 Vashti¦228649 was hosting a¦228652 celebration for the women¦228653 \add in a different part\add* of¦228654 the¦228655 royal¦228655 palace¦228654.
\rem /s1 Queen Vashti Deposed
\p
\v 10 On the¦228661 last day¦228661 of the celebrations, when the emperor¦228666 was¦228663 pleasantly tipsy, he¦228668 ordered¦228668 seven¦228676 of¦228676 \add *his\add* chief servants (Mehuman¦228669, Biztha¦228670, Harbona¦228671, Bigtha¦228672, Abagtha¦228673, Zethar¦228674, and¦228675 Karkas)
\v 11 to¦228685 bring¦228685 Queen¦228689 Vashti¦228688 to¦228690 him, wearing her¦228699 royal¦228693 crown¦228692, in order to¦228694 show¦228694 the¦228696 officials¦228696 and other people¦228695 how good-looking she was, because¦228700 she was \add very\add* beautiful¦228699.
\v 12 The¦228707 servants told her Ahasuerus's command, but Queen¦228707 Vashti¦228708 refused¦228706 to¦228709 come¦228709. When¦228715 they reported back to Ahasuerus, he¦228715 was¦228715 furious¦228715 and¦228718 \add sat there\add* raging¦228718 \add for a while\add*.
\p
\v 13-14 \add Eventually\add* \add *he\add* discussed it¦228722 with his close¦228739 advisors who¦228725 knew¦228725 the law¦228736 and¦228737 \add ≈could make good decisions¦228737\add*. (These seven¦228749 officials; Karshena, Shethar¦228743, Admatha¦228744, Tarshish¦228745, Meres¦228746, Marsena¦228747 and¦228753 Memukan; were from \add ≈various places.\add* They \add ≈advised the¦228755 emperor personally\add*, \add so were\add* the¦228756 \add ≈most powerful officials\add* in¦228759 the empire.)
\v 15 ‘According¦228761 to¦228761 \add &our¦228761\add* law¦228761,’ \add he asked,\add* ‘what¦228762 should be¦228764 done¦228764 with¦228765 Empress¦228765 Vashti¦228766, since she¦228772 refused to obey \add *my\add* command¦228775 when the servants passed it on to her?’
\p
\v 16 Memukan replied¦228782, \add addressing\add* the whole group, ‘Empress¦228795 Vashti¦228794 has done¦228793 wrong¦228793, and not only against the emperor. \add ≡She has also done wrong\add* against all¦228799 the¦228801 officials¦228801 and¦228802 people¦228806 groups of¦228810 the¦228811 empire.
\v 17 \add Soon\add* women all¦228822 over the¦228817 empire will hear about¦228819 what the¦228819 empress¦228819 did and they'll¦228828 say¦228828, “Emperor¦228829 Ahasuerus¦228830 commanded his servants to¦228832 bring¦228832 Empress Vashti¦228835 to him¦228837, but¦228838 she¦228840 didn't¦228838 go¦228816!” Then women¦228824 will stop respecting their¦228826 husbands¦228826.
\v 18 Even today, the¦228842 important wives in the¦228843 empire will¦228844 hear¦228851 what the¦228846 empress¦228855 did and¦228849 start doing the¦228854 same to¦228856 their husbands; it will¦228859 result in contempt¦228860 on her part and¦228861 anger¦228861 on his.
\v 19 If¦228863 it pleases¦228868 you \add to do so\add*, \add your majesty\add*, you should¦228869 personally issue¦228869 a¦228870 royal¦228872 decree and¦228874 have it¦228874 added to the¦228875 laws¦228875 of¦228875 Persia¦228876 and¦228878 Media¦228878 which¦228881 cannot be repealed. This decree should say that Vashti¦228885 can never again come¦228884 into¦228886 \add *your¦228888\add* presence¦228886. Then¦228889 you should¦228890 choose a different woman¦228892 for the¦228893 royal position – someone who's better¦228893 than¦228894 her¦228889.
\v 20 Have your decree¦228897 proclaimed throughout your empire, even though it's¦228896 so huge¦228906, and¦228908 then all¦228908 women¦228910, whatever their status or position, will¦228911 respect¦228912 their¦228913 husbands¦228913.’
\p
\v 21 Memukan's advice seemed like a good¦228919 idea to the¦228922 emperor¦228922 and¦228923 \add &his¦228923\add* officials¦228923, so¦228924 that's what they did¦228924.
\v 22 They wrote¦228941 letters to¦228931 every¦228933 province using its¦228941 own alphabet and¦228942 to¦228942 each¦228933 people group in its¦228946 own language¦228946. The letters said¦228953 that \add #men¦228950\add* should be¦228947 the masters over¦228952 their wives and children, and that a husband should be able to \add ≈give orders\add* to his¦228955 wife in his¦228955 own language \add and that she should understand and obey him\add*.
\c 2
\s1 Beautiful Esther enters the king's harem
\rem /s1 Esther becomes queen; Esther Becomes Queen; Esther Made Queen
\p
\v 1 Some time later¦228958 when King¦228963 Ahasuerus¦228964 \add ≈no longer felt so angry¦228962\add*, he¦228965 \add started to miss\add* Vashti¦228968. But when she'd disobeyed his command, he had made a decree \add that she could never come into his presence again\add*.
\v 2 \add ≈So¦228979\add* some of the¦228980 young¦228980 men¦228980 who¦228983 served¦228983 the¦228982 king¦228982 suggested to him¦228983, “\add Your majesty, you should get a new wife for yourself.\add* You could tell¦228979 your servants to look¦228984 for¦228985 young¦228986 virgins¦228987 who are \add very\add* beautiful.
\v 3 You could assign¦228991 officers in¦228994 each¦228994 province¦228996 of¦228996 your empire to bring every¦229001 virgin¦229005 who is \add very\add* beautiful here to your capital city of Shushan¦229010. They could stay in the harem for virgins under the custody of¦229018 Hegai¦229019, the¦229020 castrated¦229020 royal guardian who¦229022 \add ≈takes care of¦229022\add* the¦229023 young women¦229023 who live there and¦229024 he could arrange for them to receive beauty treatments.
\v 4 Then¦229027 you could decide which young¦229027 woman¦229027 you like best and¦229035 then¦229039 make¦229039 her queen instead of¦229033 Vashti¦229034.”
\p The¦229038 king¦229038 liked what they suggested, so¦229039 he¦229039 did¦229039 it.
\p
\v 5 At that time, there was a¦229043 Jewish man¦229043 named¦229048 Mordekai¦229049 living in¦229046 the capital city of Shushan¦229046. (He was from the tribe of¦229050 Benjamin: his father was Jair¦229051, his grandfather was Shimei¦229054, and his great-grandfather was Kish¦229057
\v 6 who'd¦229061 been¦229062 taken¦229062 away from¦229063 Jerusalem¦229063 and led¦229068 to¦229068 Babylon when King Nebuchadnezzer of Babylon had exiled¦229068 Jeconiah¦229070, the¦229071 king¦229071 of¦229071 Judah¦229073, along with many of¦229077 his people.)\x + \xo 2:6: \xt 2Ki 24:10-16; 2Ch 36:10.\x*
\v 7 ⇔Now \add @Mordekai\add* was \add ≈taking¦229104 care of\add* his cousin, who was an orphan. When her father and mother \add ≈had died¦229101\add*, Mordekai¦229105 had adopted her as his own daughter. Her \add Hebrew\add* name was Hadassah¦229084, and her \add Persian\add* name was¦229086 Esther. Esther was¦229091 \add now\add* a young¦229095 woman¦229095 and she was exceptionally attractive.
\p
\v 8 \add ≈So¦229109\add* when¦229110 the¦229113 king's¦229113 command and¦229114 decree became known, many¦229117 young¦229116 women¦229116 were¦229115 being brought into¦229122 the¦229124 capital city of¦229124 Shushan¦229120 and placed under Hegai's¦229125 charge, and¦229126 this is¦229126 what happened to¦229128 Esther¦229127. (Hegai¦229135 was the¦229136 overseer of¦229136 the¦229137 women¦229137.)
\v 9 \add @Hegai¦229139\add* was¦229139 very impressed with Esther, and¦229142 he treated her favourably¦229143. He¦229145 quickly¦229145 arranged for \add @Esther\add* to receive her¦229153 beauty treatments and¦229154 her allotment of food, as well as choosing¦229157 seven¦229155 female servants¦229156 from¦229161 the¦229162 king's¦229162 palace and¦229163 assigning them to be her personal attendants. He¦229163 also moved¦229163 \add @Esther¦229163\add* and¦229164 her¦229166 attendants to¦229167 the best rooms in the harem for virgins.
\v 10 (Esther¦229174 hadn't revealed her¦229177 family ties or her¦229180 Jewishness to anyone, because¦229181 Mordekai¦229182 had¦229183 instructed¦229183 her not¦229186 to.)
\p
\v 11 Every¦229190 day Mordekai¦229194 would walk¦229195 around¦229195 in¦229196 front¦229196 of¦229196 the¦229197 courtyard¦229197 of¦229197 the¦229200 women's¦229200 harem to¦229201 \add ≈find out\add* \add from others\add* how Esther¦229205 was doing¦229208 and¦229206 how she was¦229208 being treated.
\p
\v 12 ⇔Each young woman would get¦229211 twelve months of¦229222 beauty treatment according¦229224 to¦229224 the \add Persian\add* law for women¦229225—there would be¦229231 six¦229234 months¦229235 of applying oil¦229236 of¦229236 myrrh¦229237, then six months of perfumes and other lotions \add designed for\add* women. Then their turn¦229212 would come¦229215, one at a time¦229232, to be taken to King¦229219 Ahasuerus¦229220.
\v 13 The¦229245 young¦229245 woman¦229245 was¦229246 allowed to¦229247 take whatever she¦229254 wanted from¦229259 the harem when¦229244 she went to¦229261 the king's house.
\v 14 She'd be taken across in¦229266 the evening¦229266, and¦229270 in¦229270 the morning¦229270 she'd¦229271 be¦229272 led out to¦229273 the other harem which was for the¦229285 concubines¦229285. (That harem was overseen by Shaashgaz¦229281, another castrated¦229282 royal guardian.) The¦229276 women¦229276 taken there wouldn't go¦229288 again¦229289 to¦229290 the¦229292 king's¦229292 house¦229275 unless he¦229296 had especially liked them and¦229299 summoned¦229299 her back by¦229300 name¦229300.
\s1 Esther becomes queen
\p
\v 15 Now Esther¦229305 was the¦229306 daughter¦229306 of¦229306 Mordekai's uncle¦229309, Abihail¦229308, from whom¦229311 Mordekai¦229310 had¦229312 adopted her as his own daughter. When¦229302 it was her turn¦229303 to¦229316 go¦229316 to¦229317 the¦229319 king¦229319, she¦229321 didn't¦229320 ask¦229328 for anything¦229322 other than what Hegai¦229329, the¦229332 king's¦229332 trusted official \add ≈in¦229339 charge of¦229333\add* the¦229334 harem, had advised, and¦229335 everyone¦229340 who¦229342 saw¦229342 Esther liked her¦229342.
\v 16 \add ≈So¦229344\add* Esther¦229345 was¦229344 taken¦229344 to¦229346 King¦229348 Ahasuerus¦229349 at his¦229353 royal palace¦229352 in January, in the seventh year¦229360 of¦229363 his¦229363 reign¦229363,
\v 17 and¦229365 the¦229366 king¦229366 loved¦229365 Esther¦229369 more than¦229370 any of the¦229372 other women¦229372. He treated her more kindly and more favourably than¦229378 any of the¦229380 other young women \add who had become his concubines\add*. He¦229381 put¦229381 a¦229382 royal¦229384 crown¦229382 on¦229385 her¦229385 head¦229385 and¦229386 made¦229386 her¦229386 the \add new\add* queen¦229386 to replace Vashti¦229388.
\v 18 Then¦229390 the¦229391 king¦229391 hosted a¦229392 large¦229393 celebration and invited all¦229394 his¦229396 officials¦229396 and¦229397 servants¦229397 to a feast¦229399 to celebrate Esther¦229400 \add becoming queen\add*. He proclaimed that this would be a¦229401 time to celebrate for¦229402 people in all of the provinces¦229402 of his empire when they wouldn't have to pay taxes, and¦229404 he¦229404 gave¦229404 \add many\add* \add ≈generous\add* gifts.
\s1 Mordekai and Esther earn the king's favour
\rem /s1 Mordecai Discovers a Plot; Mordecai and Haman; Mordecai Saves the King's Life; Mordecai Uncovers a Conspiracy
\p
\v 19 \add Later on,\add* King Ahasuerus had \add his officers\add* bring more virgins¦229410 \add to Shushan\add*. During this time, Mordekai¦229412 \add had been given a position by the king so\add* he sat¦229413 \add there\add* at¦229414 the¦229414 king's gate¦229414.
\v 20 Esther¦229419 had still not told¦229420 anyone that \add she was Jewish\add* because Mordekai had warned her not to tell anyone. \add What's more,\add* \add *she¦229428\add* continued to follow all of¦229431 Mordekai's¦229432 instructions, just¦229435 as¦229435 she¦229436 had¦229436 done¦229434 when she was¦229436 still under his guardianship¦229437.
\rem /s1 Mordekai's Loyalty to the King
\p
\v 21 One \add #day¦229441\add* when¦229443 Mordekai¦229443 was¦229444 \add on duty\add* at¦229445 the¦229445 king's gate¦229445, two¦229451 of¦229451 the¦229454 king's¦229454 guards¦229455 who protected the¦229456 doorway¦229456 \add to the king's private rooms\add* became angry¦229448 with the¦229453 king and¦229457 so they¦229457 planned to¦229458 assassinate King¦229460 Ahasuerus¦229461. (Their names were Bigthan¦229449 and¦229450 Teresh¦229450.)
\v 22 But when Mordekai¦229465 heard what they were planning, he¦229466 told¦229466 Queen¦229468 Esther¦229470 about¦229472 it and \add *she\add* told¦229469 the¦229471 king¦229471, explaining that Mordekai¦229473 had given her the information.
\v 23 When¦229475 the¦229476 matter¦229476 was¦229477 investigated¦229475 and¦229478 \add the facts\add* discovered¦229477, both¦229479 conspirators were impaled on¦229480 \add sharpened wooden\add* poles, and in the presence¦229487 of¦229487 the¦229488 king¦229488, the incident was recorded¦229483 in¦229484 the¦229484 royal record¦229484 of events.
\c 3
\s1 Haman plans to annihilate the Jews
\rem /s1 Haman's Plot against the Jews; Haman Plots to Destroy the Jews; Haman Undertakes to Destroy the Jews; Haman's Plot to Destroy the Jews
\p
\v 1 Some time later¦229491, King¦229496 Ahasuerus¦229497 promoted¦229505 Haman¦229500, one of his officials (and the¦229501 son¦229501 of¦229501 Hammedatha¦229503 \add who was a descendant\add* of Agag). The¦229504 king gave Haman a very important position—more important than any of¦229509 his¦229509 other officials¦229513.
\v 2 Then¦229517 the¦229520 king¦229520 commanded¦229531 that¦229521 all¦229517 his other officials had to bow¦229526 down¦229526 in front of Haman¦229527 to¦229533 honour him¦229533, and they were doing this at the¦229534 king's¦229534 gate¦229523, except for¦229528 Mordekai¦229535 who¦229521 refused to bow¦229539 down¦229539 to reverence Haman.
\v 3 “Why¦229549 are¦229550 you¦229550 disobeying the¦229553 king's command¦229553?” asked¦229541 the other royal servants¦229542 at¦229546 the¦229554 king's¦229554 gate¦229546.
\v 4 ⇔\add @Mordekai\add* told¦229573 them that¦229575 it¦229556 was¦229556 because¦229571 he¦229577 was¦229578 a¦229578 Jew¦229578 \add (and Jews only worship Yahweh)\add*. He didn't¦229562 listen¦229563 to¦229564 their \add warnings\add* so¦229565 they¦229565 told¦229565 Haman¦229566 about¦229569 it to¦229567 see¦229567 if he would¦229568 tolerate¦229568 Mordekai¦229570 in keeping refusing to¦229574 bow down.
\v 5 When¦229580 Haman¦229581 saw¦229580 that¦229582 Mordekai¦229585 would not bow¦229587 down¦229587 to¦229588 him or show him reverence, \add *he¦229590\add* was¦229589 furious.
\v 6 But¦229593 \add @Haman¦229593\add* despised¦229593 the idea of¦229594 just punishing Mordekai¦229598 because¦229599 he'd been¦229599 told¦229602 that Mordekai \add didn't bow to him because\add* Mordekai was a Jew. So¦229608 Haman¦229609 decided to¦229610 annihilate¦229610 all¦229613 of¦229613 Mordekai's people, i.e., all the¦229615 Jews¦229615 throughout the¦229619 kingdom¦229619 of¦229619 Ahasuerus¦229620.
\p
\v 7 In¦229624 early April of the twelfth year¦229630 of King¦229633 Ahasuerus¦229634' reign, Haman¦229640 got his servants to¦229643 throw¦229635 dice \add to select the best month and the best day to kill the Jews\add*, and¦229644 the¦229651 13th of¦229651 March was chosen.
\p
\v 8 Then¦229655 Haman¦229656 \add went to\add* King¦229657 Ahasuerus¦229658 and said¦229655, “\add Your Majesty,\add* there¦229659 is¦229659 a¦229660 certain group of people¦229660 who live among¦229665 the¦229666 other peoples¦229666 in¦229667 every¦229667 province¦229668 of¦229668 your¦229669 empire. They have their own set of¦229669 laws¦229670, so¦229675 they don't obey your laws¦229677. It's not¦229679 good for¦229681 you to¦229685 allow them¦229685 to continue to live in your empire.
\v 9 Your majesty, if¦229687 you approve of this plan, then¦229695 write¦229693 a decree saying that all of the¦229691 \add @Jews\add* must be¦229693 destroyed¦229694. \add When they're dead, we can take all of their possessions, and from that\add* I¦229700 will¦229700 give 300 tons of¦229697 silver¦229699 to your administrators for them to put into¦229707 your royal treasuries¦229709.”
\p
\v 10 \add This seemed sensible to\add* the¦229713 king¦229713 so¦229712 he¦229712 removed¦229712 his¦229716 signet¦229716 ring from¦229717 his¦229718 finger and¦229719 he¦229719 gave¦229719 it¦229719\f + \fr 3:10 \ft Having the king's signet ring enabled Haman to stamp the king's seal (like a signature) onto the newly drafted laws.\f* to¦229720 Haman¦229720 (the¦229721 son¦229721 of¦229721 Hammedatha¦229723, the¦229724 Agagite¦229724, the¦229725 adversary of¦229725 the¦229726 Jews).
\v 11 The¦229729 king¦229729 told¦229728 Haman¦229730, “You can keep the¦229731 money¦229731 for¦229733 yourself, and you can do whatever you want to do with those people¦229734.”
\p
\v 12 On the¦229741 thirteenth day¦229747 of¦229741 that same month¦229743, Haman called¦229740 in the¦229744 royal scribes, and¦229749 he dictated a letter to¦229750 them. He¦229754 told¦229754 them to send copies to the royal officials¦229771, the¦229762 governors¦229762 of each province, and the¦229771 leaders¦229771 of¦229771 each people group \add within the empire\add*. The scribes translated the letter so it could be sent to each province using its own alphabet and to each people group in its¦229779 own language¦229779. To show that he was sending the letter under the¦229781 king's¦229781 own \add ≈authority\add*, Haman sealed¦229784 each copy of the letter with¦229785 the¦229785 ring that had the¦229786 king's¦229786 official seal on it.
\v 13 Couriers¦229791 delivered the letters to¦229792 \add the officials in\add* every¦229794 province¦229796 in the¦229796 empire. The¦229797 letters said to completely destroy¦229800 all¦229803 the¦229805 Jews¦229805, including¦229807 the children and¦229811 women¦229811, on¦229812 a single¦229813 day¦229814. That¦229820 was to be the¦229822 thirteenth day of¦229822 March early in the following year. The letters also¦229824 stated that those who killed¦229799 the Jews could take¦229825 everything that belonged to them.
\v 14 The¦229827 letter told \add the officials\add* to¦229829 display¦229835 \add #copies¦229827\add* where everyone could see them—that way all the¦229828 people¦229838 in¦229831 every¦229831 single province would know that the¦229842 king had commanded this¦229842, and they could get ready¦229840 for¦229841 when the day¦229841 came.
\p
\v 15 So couriers¦229844 hurried¦229846 off with the¦229847 letters as the¦229848 king¦229848 had commanded. A herald in the capital city¦229857 of¦229858 Shushan¦229851 also proclaimed what the¦229852 letters said. Then¦229853 the¦229853 king¦229853 and¦229854 Haman¦229854 relaxed and drank¦229856 together, but there was¦229859 \add total\add* confusion¦229859 in Shushan \add about how all of this would work out\add*.
\c 4
\s1 Mordekai asks the queen for help
\rem /s1 Mordecai Asks for Esther's Help; Esther Agrees to Help the Jews; Esther plans to rescue the Jews; Mordecai Requests Esther's Help; Mordecai Persuades Esther to Help
\p
\v 1 When¦229862 Mordekai¦229862 \add ≈found¦229863 out\add* about¦229874 what had¦229869 been¦229869 done¦229869, \add *he¦229871\add* tore¦229870 his¦229874 clothes¦229874 and¦229875 dressed in sackcloth¦229876 and¦229877 \add threw\add* ashes¦229877 \add over himself (as a sign of mourning or distress)\add* and¦229878 went¦229878 out¦229878 into¦229879 the¦229880 city¦229880 centre¦229879, wailing loudly¦229883 and¦229884 bitterly¦229884.
\v 2 But no one was wearing mourning garb was allowed inside the king's gate, so when \add @Mordekai\add* reached the gate, he¦229886 had to stay just outside it.
\v 3 In every¦229902 province of the¦229908 empire, the¦229910 letter that said to¦229915 destroy the Jews¦229915 was publicly displayed and¦229916 when¦229917 the Jews heard about it, they were incredibly upset. They skipped meals and¦229918 wailed¦229918 loudly, and many¦229902 of them also wore sackcloth¦229919 and¦229920 threw ashes¦229920 on themselves and lay on the¦229922 ground.
\p
\v 4 When¦229924 \add Queen\add* Esther's¦229929 young female attendants came¦229924 with her¦229930 guardians and¦229931 told¦229931 her¦229932 \add what was going on outside\add*, even the¦229934 queen¦229934 became very¦229935 scared. She¦229936 sent¦229936 Mordekai¦229941 some good clothes¦229938 to¦229942 wear instead of the sackcloth¦229943, but¦229945 he¦229946 wouldn't accept¦229946 them¦229946.
\v 5 The¦229952 king¦229952 had assigned some of the royal guardians to \add @Esther\add* personally, so¦229948 Esther¦229949 called¦229948 for one¦229951 of¦229951 them—a man named Hathak. She¦229956 told¦229956 him¦229956 to go out and speak with Mordekai¦229959 and \add ≈find out\add* what¦229961 he was¦229963 so¦229964 distressed about¦229964.
\v 6 \add ≈So¦229970\add* Hathak went¦229970 out¦229970 to¦229972 \add talk to\add* Mordekai¦229974 who was out in the¦229977 plaza¦229977 in¦229980 front¦229980 of¦229980 the¦229983 king's¦229983 gate¦229981.
\v 7 Mordekai¦229988 told¦229985 him¦229987 everything that¦229992 Haman was planning to¦229993 do and even told him how much money¦229997 Haman¦230000 had¦229999 said¦229999 the¦230005 king¦230005 would get for¦230006 his treasuries¦230004 if \add the king commanded people\add* to¦230009 kill¦230009 all¦229990 the¦229996 Jews.
\v 8 \add @Mordekai\add* also¦230011 gave¦230022 Hathak a copy¦230013 of¦230013 the¦230014 letter that the¦230016 announcers had¦230019 read out loud in¦230020 Shushan¦230020, that said that people must kill all the \add @Jews¦230021\add*. He told Hathak to¦230024 show¦230024 the letter to Esther¦230027 so that she would know \add exactly\add* what it said and¦230028 also¦230028 told¦230028 him to¦230029 urge her¦230029 to¦230032 go¦230032 to¦230033 the¦230035 king¦230035 personally and to beg him desperately to save her¦230043 people \add from destruction\add*.
\v 9 \add ≈So¦230045\add* Hathak returned¦230045 to the queen and¦230047 told¦230047 \add *her¦230048\add* what Mordekai¦230051 had said,
\v 10 and¦230053 Esther¦230054 told¦230053 \add *him\add* to go back to Mordekai¦230059 with this message:
\v 11 “There's a law about going to the¦230064 king¦230064 that¦230070 applies to everyone¦230071 in the kingdom, both \add #men¦230073\add* and¦230074 \add #women¦230074\add*. If anyone goes¦230076 into¦230081 the¦230084 inner¦230084 courtyard¦230083 of the¦230067 palace, where the¦230068 king¦230068 can see¦230069 them, and the¦230080 king¦230080 has not¦230086 summoned¦230088 them, that person¦230065 will be¦230089 executed¦230091. Only¦230092 if the king holds¦230094 out¦230094 his golden¦230101 scepter¦230100 to them, then¦230102 they will¦230102 live¦230102. (Everyone in the¦230112 whole empire knows¦230069 this¦230113 law.) The king hasn't called for me in over a month\add , and if I go in without being summoned, I could be put to death\add*.”
\p
\v 12 \add ≈So¦230117\add* \add Hathak\add* went back to¦230118 Mordekai¦230118 and told¦230117 him what the¦230120 queen had said.
\v 13 Mordekai¦230125 told¦230124 \add Hathak\add* to¦230126 tell this to¦230127 Esther¦230129: “Don't¦230130 imagine¦230132 that just because you live there in¦230133 the¦230137 king's¦230137 palace¦230135 that you'll be safe when¦230124 they kill all¦230138 the¦230140 other Jews¦230140.
\v 14 If you¦230146 say nothing at¦230147 all now, someone from¦230153 some other place¦230153 \add ≈will rescue¦230150\add* the Jews, but¦230155 you¦230155 and¦230156 your¦230158 relatives will¦230159 not survive. Who¦230160 knows¦230161, perhaps it was for¦230164 a¦230164 time¦230164 just like¦230165 this¦230165 that \add God allowed\add* you¦230166 to¦230167 become queen¦230167?”
\p
\v 15 Then¦230169 Esther¦230170 sent this reply to Mordekai¦230174:
\v 16 “Go¦230176 and assemble¦230177 all¦230180 the¦230182 Jews who¦230183 live here in¦230184 Shushan¦230184, and¦230185 tell them to fast¦230185 \add and pray\add* for my sake—tell them to not¦230187 eat¦230189 or drink¦230192 anything for three¦230193 days¦230194 and three nights¦230195. My female attendants and I¦230201 will¦230201 also¦230197 fast¦230201 in¦230203 the same way. At the end of the three days, I¦230204 will¦230204 go¦230204 in \add to talk\add* to¦230205 the¦230207 king¦230207 even though it's against the¦230211 law¦230211. I will do that even if it costs me my life.”
\p
\v 17 \add ≈So¦230216\add* Mordekai¦230217 went¦230216 and¦230218 did¦230218 everything that¦230220 Esther¦230224 had¦230222 told¦230222 him¦230223 to do.
\c 5
\s1 Esther goes to the king without an invitation
\rem /s1 Esther's Banquet; Esther Invites the King and Haman to a Banquet; Esther's Request to the King
\p
\v 1 Three days¦230229 later, \add Queen\add* Esther¦230232 put¦230231 on¦230231 her royal¦230233 robes and¦230234 \add went and\add* stood¦230234 in¦230235 the¦230235 inner¦230239 courtyard¦230235 of¦230235 the¦230236 palace¦230236, across from the¦230238 king's¦230238 house. He was in¦230240 the¦230241 royal palace¦230241, sitting¦230244 on¦230245 the¦230247 royal throne¦230247 and facing¦230251 the¦230252 room's entrance¦230252.
\v 2 As soon as the¦230257 king¦230257 noticed Esther¦230260 standing¦230262 there in¦230263 the courtyard¦230263, he \add ≈was very pleased\add* to see¦230256 her. So¦230267 he¦230267 held¦230267 out¦230267 his golden¦230273 scepter to¦230269 \add *her¦230269\add* and¦230276 Esther¦230277 came¦230276 up \add to the throne\add* and¦230278 touched¦230278 the¦230279 top¦230279 of¦230279 the¦230280 scepter¦230280.
\v 3 “Why have you come here, Queen¦230290 Esther¦230289?” the¦230285 king¦230285 asked. “What¦230286 do you¦230288 want? Tell me, and¦230291 I'll give¦230298 you anything you ask¦230293 for—even half¦230296 of¦230296 the¦230297 empire.”
\p
\v 4 “If¦230303 it pleases¦230308 \add %your\add* majesty,” Esther¦230302 replied¦230301, “please come¦230309 with Haman¦230311 today to¦230313 the¦230315 banquet¦230315 that¦230316 I've¦230318 prepared¦230318 for \add %you¦230319\add*.”
\p
\v 5 The king said¦230321 to his servants, “\add Go and\add* get Haman” the¦230322 king¦230322 told his servants, “and bring¦230323 him quickly¦230323 so that we can do¦230327 what the¦230330 queen has asked us.” So¦230332 the¦230333 king¦230333 and¦230334 Haman¦230334 went¦230332 to¦230335 the¦230337 banquet¦230337 that¦230338 Esther¦230341 had had prepared¦230340 for them.
\v 6 While they were drinking¦230346 wine¦230347, the¦230344 king¦230344 said¦230343 to¦230345 Esther¦230345, “\add Now\add* please tell me what¦230348 you \add really\add* want. I'll give¦230351 you anything you ask¦230355 for, no matter how much it is. I truly mean what I am saying.”
\p
\v 7 “This is what I \add really\add* want.” Esther¦230363 replied.
\v 8 “Your majesty, if¦230368 \add %you're¦230370\add* \add ≈pleased¦230379 with me\add* and¦230374 if¦230374 \add %you're¦230383\add* happy to¦230384 give¦230380 me what I want, please come¦230388 with Haman¦230390 to¦230391 \add another\add* banquet¦230393 that¦230394 I'll¦230395 prepare¦230395 for¦230396 \add %you¦230396\add* tomorrow¦230397 and I'll¦230398 answer \add %your¦230398\add* question then.”
\s1 Haman's anger with Mordekai and his execution plan
\rem /s1 Haman Plots to Kill Mordecai; Haman's Plan to Kill Mordecai; Haman Plans to Have Mordecai Hanged; Haman's Rage Against Mordecai
\p
\v 9 Haman¦230403 was feeling very happy as he¦230402 left¦230402 \add the banquet\add* that day¦230404. But¦230407 then¦230409 he saw¦230409 Mordekai¦230413 sitting at¦230414 the¦230415 king's¦230415 gate¦230414 and¦230416 noticed that \add @Mordekai¦230418\add* didn't stand¦230418 up¦230418 or show fearful reverence to him¦230422. This made Haman¦230424 furious with Mordekai¦230427
\v 10 but¦230430 \add *he¦230431\add* restrained¦230430 himself¦230430 and¦230432 went¦230432 home¦230435. Then¦230436 he¦230436 sent¦230436 for his friends¦230440 and¦230437 brought¦230437 in his¦230440 wife¦230444, Zeresh¦230443,
\v 11 boasting to¦230447 them¦230447 about how rich he was and¦230453 about¦230453 how many sons¦230454 he had, and how the king had¦230463 promoted¦230463 him \add many times\add* and given him a position above¦230464 all¦230456 of¦230456 the¦230466 other officials¦230466 and¦230467 administrators.
\v 12 “And¦230470 that is not all,” Haman¦230471 added. “I was the¦230477 only one that Queen¦230477 Esther¦230476 invited to go with¦230478 the¦230480 king¦230480 to¦230481 a banquet¦230483 that¦230484 she¦230486 prepared¦230486 for¦230487 us today. And¦230491 she's also¦230491 invited¦230495 only me to¦230497 go with¦230498 the¦230500 king¦230500 again tomorrow¦230493.
\v 13 However, I \add still\add* can't¦230505 be¦230506 happy as long as I¦230512 keep seeing¦230513 that¦230504 Jew¦230517, Mordekai¦230516, sitting¦230518 there at¦230519 the¦230520 king's¦230520 gate¦230519.”
\p
\v 14 Then¦230522 Zeresh¦230524 his¦230525 wife¦230525 and¦230526 all¦230526 his¦230528 friends¦230528 said¦230522 to¦230523 him¦230523, “Get them to install 25-metre high¦230532 pole, and¦230535 in¦230535 the morning¦230535 get permission from the¦230538 king¦230538, and¦230539 get them to impale Mordekai¦230542 on¦230543 it¦230543. Then¦230544 you can go¦230544 with¦230546 the¦230548 king¦230548 to¦230549 the¦230553 banquet¦230553 and¦230555 enjoy yourself.”
\p That idea sounded good¦230555 to¦230557 Haman¦230558, so¦230559 he¦230559 got the¦230560 pole erected.
\c 6
\s1 The king honours Mordekai
\rem /s1 Haman executed; The King Honours Mordecai; Mordecai Honoured
\p
\v 1 That¦230564 night¦230563 the¦230566 king was unable to sleep¦230566 so¦230568 he¦230568 ordered¦230568 the royal records to be fetched and¦230576 to be read¦230577 to¦230578 him.
\v 2 The records¦230582 mentioned that¦230583 Bigthan and¦230589 Teresh¦230589, two¦230590 of¦230590 the royal guardians¦230593 who¦230595 protected the¦230594 doorway¦230594 to the king's private quarters, had¦230596 planned to¦230597 assassinate the king¦230599, but that Mordekai¦230585 had¦230584 reported¦230584 it.
\v 3 “What¦230604 honour¦230607 or recognition did I give Mordekai¦230609 for saving my life?” the king asked.
\p “Nothing was¦230606 done¦230606 for¦230609 him,” replied the¦230614 young¦230614 men¦230614 who¦230616 were¦230619 attending him¦230620.
\p
\v 4 At that¦230625 moment, Haman¦230627 had¦230628 entered¦230628 the¦230629 outer¦230633 courtyard¦230629 of¦230629 the¦230630 king's house¦230630. He had come¦230628 to¦230634 tell¦230634 the¦230635 king¦230635 that he wanted to¦230636 execute Mordekai¦230639 on¦230640 the¦230642 pole that¦230643 he'd¦230645 had¦230645 set¦230645 up for him¦230646.
\p “Who's¦230625 out in¦230626 the courtyard?” the¦230632 king¦230632 asked¦230634.
\p
\v 5 “Haman's¦230653 out there¦230652 standing¦230654 in¦230655 the¦230649 courtyard¦230655,” replied¦230648 the¦230650 young men.
\p “Bring him¦230651 in then¦230656,” the¦230657 king¦230657 instructed.
\p
\v 6 When¦230660 Haman¦230661 came¦230660 in, the¦230664 king¦230664 asked him, “What¦230665 should I do¦230667 for¦230668 a man¦230668 that I would really like to honour¦230672?”
\p Haman¦230674 thought to himself, “Surely it must¦230677 be me that the¦230678 king¦230678 would like to honour¦230680 more¦230682 than¦230682 anyone else?”
\v 7 \add ≈so¦230684\add* \add *he¦230685\add* replied¦230684 to¦230686 the king, “If \add %you¦230692\add* really want to honour¦230693 someone,
\v 8 tell your servants to bring¦230695 one of¦230696 your own royal¦230697 robes that¦230698 \add %you¦230699\add* have¦230699 already worn¦230699 yourself and¦230703 also¦230703 \add ≡bring¦230695\add* a¦230703 horse¦230703 that¦230704 \add %you¦230705\add* have¦230705 already ridden¦230705 yourself and¦230708 put¦230709 a¦230710 royal¦230711 crown on¦230712 its¦230712 head¦230712.
\v 9 Then have one¦230722 of¦230722 \add %your\add* most noble¦230724 officials¦230722 present the man with the robe and the horse. Have your servants put¦230714 the¦230715 robe on¦230717 the man whom¦230729 \add %you¦230731\add* really want to honour¦230732 and¦230733 have them seat that man on¦230734 the horse and then lead the horse through the public square of¦230737 the¦230738 city¦230738 while they¦230739 shout out \add to everyone\add* \add ≈in¦230740 front¦230740 of¦230740\add* them, ‘The king is¦230742 doing¦230742 this because¦230743 he¦230746 really wants to honour¦230747 this man!’ ”
\p
\v 10 The¦230750 king¦230750 \add agreed so\add* he¦230749 replied¦230749 to¦230751 Haman¦230751, “Go quickly¦230752 and take¦230753 the¦230756 robe and¦230757 the¦230759 horse¦230759 and¦230762 do¦230762 what you've just¦230760 described for¦230765 Mordekai¦230765 the¦230766 Jew¦230766 who¦230767 sits¦230767 by the¦230768 gate¦230768 to the¦230769 palace. Make¦230772 sure that¦230775 you¦230776 do absolutely everything that you've said¦230776.”
\p
\v 11 \add ≈So¦230778\add* Haman¦230779 got¦230778 the¦230782 robe and¦230783 the¦230785 horse¦230785 and¦230786 put the robe on Mordekai¦230789, seated him on the horse, and then led the horse through the public square of¦230791 the¦230792 city¦230792. As he did, he shouted¦230793 out to everyone \add ≈in¦230794 front¦230794 of¦230794\add* him¦230794, “The¦230799 king¦230799 is¦230796 doing¦230796 this because¦230797 he¦230800 really wants to honour¦230801 this man¦230797!”
\p
\v 12 Afterwards, Mordekai¦230804 returned¦230803 to¦230805 the¦230808 king's¦230808 gate¦230807 but¦230809 Haman¦230809 hurried¦230810 to¦230811 his¦230813 house¦230813, covering¦230815 \add &his\add* head¦230816 like a mourner¦230814.
\v 13 \add At home\add* \add *he¦230819\add* explained to¦230820 Zeresh¦230820 his¦230821 wife¦230821 and¦230822 to¦230822 all¦230822 his¦230824 friends¦230824 everything that¦230828 had¦230829 happened¦230829 to¦230829 him¦230829. Then his advisors and¦230830 his wife said¦230830 to¦230832 him¦230832, “Mordekai¦230839 has¦230841 begun¦230841 to¦230842 defeat you and¦230834 since he is¦230837 one¦230837 of¦230837 the Jewish people, you won't¦230844 win against him¦230847—instead, he'll certainly¦230850 defeat you¦230851.”
\s1 Haman's execution
\rem /s1 Haman Is Put to Death; Haman's Downfall and Mordecai's Advancement
\p
\v 14 While they¦230854 were¦230855 still¦230854 talking¦230855 together, \add some of\add* the royal guardians arrived¦230859 to¦230861 bring¦230861 Haman¦230864 quickly¦230860 to¦230865 the¦230867 banquet¦230867 that¦230868 Esther¦230871 had had prepared¦230870.
\c 7
\rem /s1 The King Executes Haman; Haman Hanged
\p
\v 1 \add ≈So¦230873\add* the¦230874 king¦230874 and¦230875 Haman¦230875 went¦230873 to¦230876 Queen¦230880 Esther's¦230879 \add banquet\add*.
\v 2 While they were drinking¦230888 wine¦230889 at that second¦230887 banquet, the¦230883 king¦230883 asked¦230882 Esther¦230884 again, “\add Now\add* please tell me what¦230890 you \add really\add* want, Queen¦230894 Esther. Tell me, and¦230895 I'll do it¦230895 for¦230896 you—I'll give¦230895 you anything you ask for, no matter what¦230897 it¦230904 is¦230904.”
\p
\v 3 “Your¦230915 majesty¦230915,” Queen¦230908 Esther¦230907 replied, “if¦230916 you \add ≈are¦230918 pleased¦230921 with me\add*, I hope you'll be¦230922 willing to¦230924 do what I ask. Please allow¦230922 me¦230924 to \add ≈live¦230925\add*, and¦230927 please save my¦230927 people¦230927. That is what I am asking for¦230928
\v 4 because¦230930 someone has¦230931 turned me and¦230933 my¦230933 people¦230933 over \add to our enemies\add* and¦230936 they're going to¦230936 completely destroy¦230936 us. If¦230937 someone had \add just\add* sold the men and¦230939 even the women to¦230939 be slaves¦230939, I¦230941 wouldn't have said anything, because¦230942 that wouldn't have been¦230945 important enough to bother your¦230947 majesty¦230947 with.”
\p
\v 5 “Who has done this?” King¦230951 Ahasuerus¦230952 asked¦230953 \add *her¦230954\add*. “Where¦230959 is¦230962 the man who¦230963 has¦230965 dared to do¦230967 such a thing?”
\p
\v 6 “The man¦230973 who is \add &our\add* bitter enemy¦230975 is this¦230978 evil¦230977 \add >man\add* Haman¦230976!” answered¦230970 Esther¦230972.
\p \add By this time,\add* Haman¦230979 was¦230980 terrified¦230980 standing there in front¦230981 of the¦230982 king¦230982 and¦230983 the¦230983 queen¦230983.
\v 7 Now the¦230985 king¦230985 \add ≈became so angry¦230987\add* that he¦230986 got up¦230986 and abandoned his¦230987 wine-drinking at the¦230988 banquet¦230988 and went outside into¦230990 the¦230993 palace¦230993 garden¦230992. But¦230994 Haman¦230994 stayed¦230995 inside to¦230996 beg his¦230999 life¦230999 from¦231000 Queen¦231001 Esther¦231000 because¦231002 he¦231003 recognized that¦231004 the king wanted to¦231007 \add execute\add* him¦231007.
\v 8 \add As he was pleading for his life,\add* Haman¦231022 \add knelt down\add* very close to Esther¦231028 as she was \add reclining\add* on a couch¦231026. When¦231012 the¦231012 king¦231012 returned¦231013 from¦231014 the¦231015 palace¦231015 garden¦231014 to¦231016 the room where they had been drinking¦231020 wine¦231021, he \add saw this and\add* exclaimed, “\add Now\add* he's¦231030 even trying to¦231033 rape¦231033 the¦231036 queen¦231036 in my presence and in my own house¦231038!”
\p As soon as the¦231042 king¦231042 said¦231030 this, some of¦231043 his servants covered¦231045 Haman's¦231044 face¦231043 \add (as a sign that he would be executed)\add*.
\v 9 Then¦231048 Harbona¦231049, who was one¦231050 of¦231051 the guardians who served the¦231055 king¦231055 personally, said¦231066, “Your¦231071 majesty¦231071, Haman¦231074 has also¦231056 set up a pole twenty-five metres high¦231075 at his house¦231073 because he¦231078 wants to hang¦231080 Mordekai¦231064 on¦231069 it. But \add @Mordekai\add* saved your life.”
\p “Execute Haman on¦231081 it¦231081!” the king commanded.
\p
\v 10 \add ≈So¦231083\add* they¦231083 impaled Haman¦231086 on¦231087 the¦231089 pole that¦231090 he¦231092 had¦231092 set¦231092 up for¦231093 Mordekai¦231093, and¦231094 then the¦231095 king's¦231095 anger¦231094 dissipated.
\c 8
\s1 The Jews are enabled to defend themselves
\rem /s1 Esther Saves the Jews; The King's Edict in Behalf of the Jews; A Decree to Help the Jews; The Jews Are Told to Fight Back
\p
\v 1 That same day¦231099, King¦231102 Ahasuerus¦231103 gave¦231101 Queen¦231105 Esther¦231104 all the¦231108 property that had belonged to Haman¦231109, the¦231110 enemy of¦231110 the¦231111 Jews¦231111. Esther¦231120 told¦231119 the¦231116 king¦231116 that \add @Mordekai¦231119\add* was¦231122 \add her cousin and that he had been like a father\add* to¦231124 her¦231124, and \add so the king summoned\add* Mordekai¦231113 to come¦231114 to¦231115 him.
\v 2 Then¦231126 the¦231127 king¦231127 removed¦231126 his¦231130 signet¦231130 ring which¦231131 he¦231132 had¦231132 retrieved from¦231133 Haman¦231133, and¦231134 gave¦231134 it¦231134 to¦231135 Mordekai¦231135. The queen also¦231136 put Mordekai¦231140 \add ≈in charge¦231136 of\add* all the property that had belonged to Haman¦231144.
\rem /s1 The triumphant Jews destroy their enemies
\p
\v 3 Then¦231147 Esther¦231148 came to speak¦231149 to¦231150 the¦231151 king¦231151 again, and¦231152 she¦231152 knelt¦231152 down and put her face¦231153 right on top of¦231153 his¦231154 feet¦231154. She¦231155 cried¦231155 as she¦231156 begged him¦231158 to¦231159 stop Haman's¦231163 terrible plan¦231166 to destroy the¦231171 Jews¦231171.
\v 4 The¦231174 king¦231174 held¦231173 out¦231173 his golden¦231178 scepter¦231177 toward Esther¦231180, so¦231181 \add *she\add* stood¦231181 up and waited facing¦231182 the¦231183 king¦231183.
\v 5 “Your majesty,” she¦231185 said¦231185, “if you think that it's¦231197 the¦231198 right¦231197 thing to¦231199 do, and¦231201 if you're \add ≈pleased¦231201 with me\add*, please write¦231204 a new letter revoking¦231205 the¦231208 letters¦231208 that Haman¦231210 (the¦231211 son¦231211 of¦231211 Hammedatha¦231213, the¦231214 Agagite¦231214) sent out \add instructing the people\add* to¦231217 destroy¦231217 all¦231222 the¦231220 Jews¦231220 throughout your empire.
\v 6 I couldn't bear to see¦231230 the terrible thing that¦231232 is about¦231237 to happen to my¦231237 people¦231237. I \add certainly\add* couldn't bear to see¦231240 \add all\add* my¦231242 relatives¦231242 \add ≈destroyed¦231241\add*!”
\p
\v 7 Then¦231245 king¦231246 Ahasuerus¦231247 said¦231245 to¦231248 Queen¦231249 Esther¦231248 and¦231250 to¦231250 Mordekai¦231250 the¦231251 Jew¦231251, “Listen, I've¦231256 given¦231256 the¦231253 house¦231253 of¦231253 Haman¦231255 to¦231257 Esther¦231257, and¦231258 they've¦231259 executed him¦231258 on¦231260 the¦231262 pole because he¦231266 attempted to destroy the Jews.
\v 8 But¦231271 as you¦231271 \add both\add* know, no one can revoke¦231296 a letter that¦231286 has my name¦231278 and¦231280 my official seal¦231280 on it. So this is¦231288 what you should do: write¦231288 \add a new letter\add* to help the¦231291 Jews, as you think best and¦231292 put my name on it¦231292 and seal¦231292 the letter with my signet¦231293 ring.”
\p
\v 9 It was now June, and¦231298 the¦231301 king¦231301 sent for his scribes¦231299 and they¦231298 wrote¦231314 a letter to the Jews containing everything that¦231317 Mordekai¦231320 dictated. It was also addressed to¦231321 royal officials¦231328, and¦231324 to¦231324 the¦231327 governors¦231327 and¦231328 leaders¦231328 in each province. The¦231329 empire had 127 provinces¦231329, extending all¦231315 the way from¦231332 India¦231332 \add in the east\add* to¦231333 Ethiopia¦231335 \add in the west\add*. \add ≡The scribes wrote\add* to every province using its own alphabet and to each people group in its¦231345 own language¦231345, and¦231346 to¦231346 the¦231348 Jews¦231348 in their¦231349 language and¦231350 using their¦231350 alphabet.
\v 10 The letters were written¦231352 in¦231353 King¦231354 Ahasuerus¦231355' name¦231353, and¦231356 \add each one\add* was sealed¦231356 with¦231357 the¦231357 king's signet¦231357 ring. Then the¦231358 letters¦231360 were distributed by¦231361 mounted couriers¦231362 riding¦231364 on¦231363 swift horses¦231363 bred from the¦231366 royal¦231366 mares¦231368.
\p
